WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.780
Prompt history is sensitive operational data.

2
00:00:03.880 --> 00:00:08.760
An AI interaction record can contain much more than the user's typed prompt.

3
00:00:08.860 --> 00:00:25.660
It may include the response, uploaded files, retrieved documents, generated outputs, tool calls, system messages, plugin results, model identifiers, user identifiers, timestamps, feedback, error traces, and session metadata.

4
00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:39.440
Those records can reveal business strategy, customer issues, internal investigations, product plans, source code, credentials accidentally pasted by users, regulated information, or personal data.

5
00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:41.800
Prompt history can also be useful.

6
00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:51.900
It helps users resume work, helps support teams debug problems, helps product teams understand quality, and helps security teams investigate unusual behavior.

7
00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:56.560
It can support evaluation, troubleshooting, and reliability improvement.

8
00:00:56.660 --> 00:01:02.340
The operational value is real, which is why many systems store more than users realize.

9
00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:07.900
Usefulness does not justify unlimited collection or indefinite retention.

10
00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:16.000
Every retained record creates responsibilities around access, storage, deletion, auditability, and reuse.

11
00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:25.300
A record saved for user continuity may later be copied into logs, analytics systems, evaluation datasets, exports, or backups.

12
00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:28.840
Each copy expands the lifecycle the organization must manage.

13
00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:37.360
Good prompt-history governance starts with a simple question: what are we keeping, why are we keeping it, who can see it, and when should it expire?

14
00:01:37.460 --> 00:01:46.100
Treating prompt history as a lifecycle problem helps teams design AI systems that are useful, supportable, and respectful of privacy and confidentiality.
